<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.11.4: Maximum, Minimum, and Analyzing the Vertex</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02514</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.11.4: Maximum, Minimum, and Analyzing the Vertex</md:title>
    <md:uuid>50a75964-077d-4ed5-9a30-5a4341f9de5b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Ask students to put away their devices for the cool down.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Without graphing, find the vertex (of a graph) of the graph of a quadratic function defined by \(f(x)=-x^2−14x−60\). Show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The vertex is at \((-7,-11)\).</para>
<para>\(f(x)=-(x^2+14x+60)\)</para>
<para>\(f(x)=-(x^2+14x+49+11)\)</para>
<para>\(f(x)=-((x+7)^2+11)\)</para>
<para>\(f(x)=-(x+7)^2−11\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Does the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex correspond to a maximum or a minimum value of the function? Explain how you know.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> A maximum value. The squared term has a coefficient of -1. The squared term will be 0 when \(x\) is -7. For all other values of \(x\), the squared term will be subtracted from -11, resulting in outputs that are less than -11.</para>
<para><emphasis>Note: </emphasis>If the vertex isn't asked for and the "value of the maximum or minimum" is asked for, the maximum or minimum value occurs at the \(y\)-value of the vertex because that is the value of the coordinate that represents the vertical height of the point.</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>If students struggle to transform quadratic expressions into vertex form, and it is emphasized in your standards, take a few minutes from the next lesson to highlight examples of misconceptions from student work and use practice problems for additional opportunities to practice.</para></section>
</content>
